995 {"goal": "When a set of changes caused a score regression, how can the pipeline be stabilized without fully reverting to the old version?", "context": "Multiple changes were bundled (augmentation, architecture tweak, checkpoint) and the combined effect was negative. A full revert would lose any beneficial sub-changes.", "action": "Remove dead code and unused dependencies to reduce noise, add robustness measures like early stopping and averaging predictions across cross-validation folds, and re-submit. Fold averaging smooths out variance and can partially compensate for a suboptimal single-fold configuration."}
INSTANCE {"goal": "After a change worsened the score, how can the pipeline be cleaned up and partially recovered without reverting entirely?", "context": "v105 scored 0.28170 (regression from 0.27315). The author cleaned up: removed unused imports (librosa, albumentations), removed dead mel-spectrogram code, simplified augmentation, added early stopping, and averaged fold predictions. v107 scored 0.27969, partially recovering.", "action": "Rather than a full revert, the author simplified the pipeline (removed unused dependencies and dead code), added fold-prediction averaging and early stopping, and pointed to a new checkpoint. This recovered most of the regression (0.28170 → 0.27969) though not fully to 0.27315."}
1039 {"goal": "How much can the score improve by switching from a generic pretrained image model to domain-specific architectures with proper signal-processing features?", "context": "A generic vision backbone applied to spectrograms gives a weak baseline on a physiological time-series classification task. The raw signals are available and domain knowledge suggests filtering and specialized temporal models are far more effective.", "action": "Replace the generic image backbone with an ensemble of domain-specific models (e.g., CNN-RNN on raw signals) that incorporate proper signal preprocessing (bandpass filtering, custom spectrograms). Load multiple fold checkpoints and average their predictions. Expect a large score jump—potentially halving the error or more—because the architecture and features now match the data modality."}
1040 {"goal": "When a weak baseline needs a large improvement, is it better to make incremental changes or bundle multiple high-confidence improvements together?", "context": "The baseline is far from competitive, and several improvements (domain-appropriate architecture, proper signal preprocessing, ensembling) are each individually well-supported by known strong solutions. Testing each one separately would be slow.", "action": "When the gap to competitive performance is large and multiple improvements are each strongly motivated by domain knowledge or known solutions, bundle them into a single refactor. Verify the combined effect on the leaderboard. Reserve incremental ablation for later fine-tuning once the score is in a competitive range."}
1068 {"goal": "After isolating a single component reveals it performs far below the ensemble baseline, how should the pipeline be restructured to recover?", "context": "A diagnostic test (submitting one model alone) showed that the individual model is much weaker than the blended ensemble, indicating the pipeline's feature engineering and validation strategy need improvement rather than just re-tuning blend weights.", "action": "Refactor the data and feature pipeline: add domain-appropriate signal preprocessing (e.g., filtering, derived features), switch to a grouped stratified cross-validation scheme that respects the data's group structure, and rebuild the out-of-fold and inference flow before returning to ensemble tuning. This addresses root-cause weaknesses rather than superficial weight adjustments."}
INSTANCE {"goal": "After a single-model submission scored very poorly (0.68), how can the pipeline be restructured to recover and improve performance?", "context": "The previous step (v8) isolated a single model's predictions (predss_1) for submission, which scored 0.68 — far worse than the earlier ensemble score of 0.30. This indicated the single model alone was inadequate and the pipeline needed fundamental changes.", "action": "Performed a major refactor: disabled the existing Keras data generator, reorganized the feature pipeline to incorporate bandpass filtering, EEG montage features, and spectrogram features, switched validation to StratifiedGroupKFold with a custom metric, and restructured the OOF/inference flow. This recovered to LB 0.36, much better than the single-model 0.68 but not yet matching the best ensemble at 0.30."}
1096 {"goal": "When iterating over saved checkpoints of the same architecture has plateaued, where can further gain come from?", "context": "Many checkpoint swaps have been tried without surpassing an early best score. The task is multi-label, and the current model uses a single shared classification head for all labels.", "action": "Switch from a single shared head to a multi-head architecture with per-group spatial attention modules, so each label group gets a dedicated prediction pathway. Load backbone weights from a strong pretrained or distilled model and fine-tune the new heads. This changes the modeling capacity rather than just selecting among existing weight snapshots."}
1180 {"goal": "How much can inference quality improve when upgrading the data pipeline, increasing input resolution, and switching to a stronger checkpoint simultaneously?", "context": "The current inference pipeline uses a legacy data-loading API at lower resolution with a weaker checkpoint, and the task benefits from fine spatial detail in images.", "action": "Replace the legacy image-loading pipeline with the framework's optimized data pipeline, increase input resolution substantially, and swap in a stronger pretrained checkpoint. Expect that the resolution increase and better checkpoint together can yield a large score jump (several AUC points), though the individual contributions are confounded."}
INSTANCE {"goal": "How much can the score improve by switching from a slow ImageDataGenerator pipeline to a tf.data pipeline, increasing image resolution from lower to 512, and loading a better model checkpoint?", "context": "Previous LB was 0.88199 using Keras ImageDataGenerator with a certain model checkpoint. The competition involves multi-label classification on chest X-rays where higher resolution helps capture catheter/line details.", "action": "Replaced ImageDataGenerator with a tf.data pipeline (build_decoder, build_augmenter, build_dataset), added TPU/accelerator strategy auto-selection, increased img_size to 512, and loaded a different TF model checkpoint. This yielded a +0.07 jump to LB 0.95180."}
1211 {"goal": "When extensive in-notebook feature generation has become a source of fragile tuning, can moving that computation into the model or using precomputed features simplify the pipeline?", "context": "Multiple recent edits tuning feature-generation parameters (window sizes, cropping) have failed to improve scores and introduced complexity. The feature generation code is large and tightly coupled to the inference loop.", "action": "Remove the in-notebook feature generation code and either use precomputed features or move the transform into the model's forward pass (e.g., using a differentiable spectrogram layer). Accept a temporary score dip from the refactor, then add the replacement model in the next step."}
1236 {"goal": "When the ensemble composition is being adjusted, can simultaneously tuning the spectrogram resolution yield additional gains?", "context": "A time-frequency spectrogram is generated from raw signals and fed to pretrained image models. The hop length controls temporal resolution and the crop controls which portion of the spectrogram the model sees.", "action": "When making an ensemble change that is expected to improve the score, bundle a small spectrogram parameter tweak (e.g., halving the hop length for finer time resolution, tightening the crop) in the same submission. If the combined change improves, keep both; if not, revert the spectrogram change separately to isolate effects."}
INSTANCE {"goal": "Can adjusting the spectrogram hop_length and image crop offset further improve the score alongside the ensemble change?", "context": "At v74 (LB 0.28218), the spectrogram used hop_length=50 and a +20 pixel crop offset. The author suspected finer time resolution and a tighter crop could help.", "action": "Halved hop_length from 50 to 25 (doubling time resolution) and reduced the crop offset from +20 to +10 pixels simultaneously with removing the 3D ResNet from the ensemble. The combined change improved LB from 0.28218 to 0.27839."}
